% =============================================================================
% PART I: PROJECT NARRATIVE
% =============================================================================

\part{Project Genesis and Design Decisions}

% =============================================================================
\section{How This Project Came About}
\label{sec:genesis}
% =============================================================================

This project is a collaboration between a PhD student in statistics (based in
Iceland, specialising in Bayesian latent Gaussian models) and his nephew, a
geology professor at the University of M\'{a}laga who works on hydrogeological
hazards in southern Spain.

The motivation is direct: \textbf{rainfall, floods, and droughts} are problems
of immediate concern to the people and infrastructure of M\'{a}laga and
Andaluc\'{\i}a. Recent DANA (Depresi\'{o}n Aislada en Niveles Altos) events
have caused devastating floods in eastern Spain, making probabilistic extreme
rainfall analysis a topic of urgent practical relevance.

Bayesian latent Gaussian models with spatial random effects are a powerful
framework for modelling extremes of precipitation. The key reference is the
final chapter of \citet{hrafnkelsson2023blgm}, which presents a BLGM for
high-dimensional spatial extremes of daily rainfall --- methodology developed
within our own research group at the University of Iceland.

% =============================================================================
\section{The Problem: Extreme Precipitation in M\'{a}laga and Andaluc\'{\i}a}
\label{sec:problem}
% =============================================================================

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Why This Matters}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

M\'{a}laga province and the broader Andaluc\'{\i}a region are characterised by
a Mediterranean climate with pronounced seasonal contrasts: long dry summers
and a wet season concentrated in autumn--winter. This climatology creates a
dual hazard:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Extreme rainfall and floods.} Short-duration, high-intensity
    precipitation events --- often triggered by DANA (``gota fr\'{\i}a'')
    systems --- can deliver a significant fraction of the annual rainfall total
    in a matter of hours. The steep, short river basins draining the coastal
    sierras into the Mediterranean respond rapidly, producing flash floods with
    little warning time.

  \item \textbf{Droughts.} Multi-year precipitation deficits are a recurring
    feature of the regional climate, with severe consequences for agriculture,
    municipal water supply, and aquifer recharge. The Guadalhorce and
    Guadalmedina basins serving M\'{a}laga are particularly vulnerable.
\end{enumerate}

For both hazards, decision-makers need \emph{probabilistic} answers: not just
``how much rain can we expect?'' but ``how much rain, with what probability,
and how confident are we?'' Classical frequency analysis provides point
estimates of return levels at individual stations but cannot:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Borrow strength across nearby stations to improve estimates at
    data-sparse locations,
  \item Predict return levels at ungauged sites (spatial interpolation),
  \item Quantify the full posterior uncertainty in return level estimates,
  \item Incorporate covariates (elevation, distance to coast, orographic
    exposure) in a principled way.
\end{itemize}

A Bayesian spatial extreme value model addresses all four limitations.

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{The DANA Phenomenon}
\label{sec:dana}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

DANA events (historically called ``gota fr\'{\i}a'') occur when a pocket of
cold air at upper levels (typically 500\,hPa) becomes detached from the polar
jet stream and drifts southward over the western Mediterranean. When this cold
upper-level air mass encounters warm, moisture-laden air over the sea surface,
the resulting instability drives intense convective precipitation.

\todo{Add specific recent DANA events affecting M\'{a}laga/Andaluc\'{\i}a
with dates and recorded precipitation totals. Jes\'{u}s to provide local
context.}

% =============================================================================
\section{Research Questions}
\label{sec:questions}
% =============================================================================

If successful, this project will produce Bayesian return level maps with full
posterior uncertainty for extreme rainfall across M\'{a}laga and
Andaluc\'{\i}a. Specifically, we aim to answer:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Spatial return levels.} What are the 20-, 50-, and 100-year
    return levels for daily precipitation at any location in the study area,
    including ungauged sites?

  \item \textbf{Uncertainty mapping.} How uncertain are these return level
    estimates? Where is uncertainty highest --- and where might additional
    monitoring stations reduce it most?

  \item \textbf{Drivers of spatial variation.} How do the GEV parameters
    (location~$\mu$, scale~$\sigma$, shape~$\xi$) depend on geographic
    covariates such as elevation, distance to coast, and orographic exposure?

  \item \textbf{Infrastructure adequacy.} How do estimated return levels
    compare against the rainfall thresholds used for drainage design, dam
    sizing, and civil protection planning in M\'{a}laga?

  \item \textbf{Benchmark against classical methods.} How much does the
    Bayesian spatial model improve upon traditional at-site GEV fits that
    ignore spatial structure?
\end{enumerate}

% =============================================================================
\section{Methodological Approach}
\label{sec:approach}
% =============================================================================

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Overview}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

We adopt a \textbf{Bayesian latent Gaussian model} for spatial extremes,
following the framework of \citet{hazra2023blgm}. Instead of annual block
maxima, we model \textbf{daily threshold exceedances} via the Poisson point
process (PPP) likelihood, which provides a unified framework for both the
exceedance rate and the tail behaviour.

Inference proceeds via the \textbf{Max-and-Smooth} algorithm
\citep{hrafnkelsson2021maxsmooth}:

\begin{description}[style=nextline, labelwidth=3cm, leftmargin=3.5cm]
  \item[\textbf{Stage~1 (Max)}] At each station, fit the Poisson point process
    model independently to obtain MLEs of the transformed GEV parameters
    $(\psi_i, \tau_i, \phi_i)$ and their covariance matrices (via parametric
    bootstrap).

  \item[\textbf{Stage~2 (Smooth)}] Treat the site-wise MLEs as data in a
    Gaussian surrogate likelihood model with latent Gaussian spatial effects
    (Mat\'{e}rn GP). Fit via MCMC (Stan/NUTS) to obtain spatially smoothed
    posteriors for all parameters at every station.
\end{description}

The three-level hierarchical structure is:

\begin{description}[style=nextline, labelwidth=3cm, leftmargin=3.5cm]
  \item[\textbf{Data layer}] Daily precipitation threshold exceedances at
    each station, modelled by the PPP likelihood with station-specific
    GEV parameters $(\mu_i, \sigma_i, \xi_i)$.

  \item[\textbf{Latent layer}] Transformed parameters
    $\psi_i = \log(\mu_i)$, $\tau_i = \log(\sigma_i / \mu_i)$,
    $\phi_i = h(\xi_i)$ are linked to intercepts plus Mat\'{e}rn(5/2)
    Gaussian process spatial random effects and nuggets (for all three
    parameters).

  \item[\textbf{Prior layer}] Penalised complexity (PC) priors
    \citep{simpson2017pc, fuglstad2019pc} on the GP hyperparameters
    (range, marginal standard deviation) and nugget standard deviations.
\end{description}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Why Max-and-Smooth with PPP, Not INLA with GEV}
\label{sec:ms_vs_inla}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Two inference strategies are available within the BLGM framework:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{R-INLA + GEV block maxima} \citep{rue2009inla}: Fits a
    hierarchical GEV model on annual maxima in a single coherent inference
    step. Uses only one observation per station per year.

  \item \textbf{Max-and-Smooth + PPP} \citep{hrafnkelsson2021maxsmooth,
    hazra2023blgm}: Two-stage inference on daily threshold exceedances via
    the Poisson point process likelihood. Stage~1 computes site-wise MLEs;
    Stage~2 smooths them spatially via MCMC.
\end{enumerate}

We choose \textbf{Max-and-Smooth with the PPP likelihood} because:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The PPP likelihood uses \emph{all} daily exceedances above a
    threshold, not just one observation per year. This extracts far more
    information from the tail and yields more precise estimates, especially
    for the shape parameter~$\xi$.
  \item The PPP provides a unified model for both the exceedance rate and
    the tail behaviour (unlike a GP + Bernoulli split, which would require
    two separate models).
  \item R-INLA does not directly support the PPP likelihood; M\&S provides
    a natural MCMC-based inference framework for it.
  \item The methodology follows exactly Chapter~7 of \citet{hrafnkelsson2023blgm},
    providing a direct application of research from our group to a new domain.
\end{itemize}

\designnote{An INLA-based analysis using annual block maxima (GEV likelihood)
could serve as a complementary comparison. The M\&S + PPP approach is
preferred as the primary method due to its more efficient use of the data.}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Why Station Data, Not Gridded Products}
\label{sec:station_vs_grid}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Several gridded precipitation products exist for Spain (AEMET ROCIO, SPREAD,
Spain02, Iberia01, ERA5-Land). However, all gridded products involve
interpolation or model-based estimation, which tends to \textbf{smooth the
tails} of the precipitation distribution --- exactly the part we care about
for extreme value analysis.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{AEMET ROCIO} (5\,km): Optimal interpolation of station data.
    Grid cell values are statistical estimates, not direct readings.
  \item \textbf{SPREAD} (5\,km): Geostatistical interpolation with uncertainty
    estimates. Better than ROCIO for our purposes, but still smoothed.
  \item \textbf{ERA5 / ERA5-Land}: Reanalysis (numerical weather model output).
    Documented to underestimate heavy ($\geq 20$\,mm/day) and violent
    ($\geq 40$\,mm/day) precipitation over Spain.
\end{itemize}

We therefore use \textbf{raw AEMET rain gauge measurements} as our response
variable. The spatial GP in the Bayesian model handles interpolation to
ungauged locations in a principled way, without pre-smoothing the extremes.

Gridded products may still serve as covariates or for spatial context (e.g.,
ERA5 large-scale atmospheric fields as predictors of local extremes).

% =============================================================================
\section{Model Specification}
\label{sec:model}
% =============================================================================

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Notation}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Let $y_{ij}$ denote the $j$-th daily precipitation exceeding a threshold $u_i$
at station~$i$ ($i = 1, \ldots, n$), and let $n_i^{\mathrm{ex}}$ denote the
number of exceedances at station~$i$ over $n_T$ total observation days.
Let $\mathbf{s}_i \in \mathbb{R}^2$ denote the spatial coordinates of
station~$i$.

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Data Model: Poisson Point Process Likelihood}
\label{sec:data_model}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Daily precipitation exceedances follow a Poisson point process (PPP) model.
The PPP log-likelihood for station~$i$ with threshold~$u_i$, GEV
parameters~$(\mu_i, \sigma_i, \xi_i)$, and $n_{py} = 365.25$ days per year
is:
%
\begin{equation}
  \ell_i(\mu_i, \sigma_i, \xi_i) =
    -n_i^{\mathrm{ex}} \log \sigma_i
    - \left(\frac{1}{\xi_i} + 1\right) \sum_{j=1}^{n_i^{\mathrm{ex}}}
      \log\!\left[ 1 + \xi_i \frac{y_{ij} - \mu_i}{\sigma_i} \right]
    - \frac{n_T}{n_{py}} \left[ 1 + \xi_i \frac{u_i - \mu_i}{\sigma_i}
      \right]^{-1/\xi_i}
  \label{eq:ppp_loglik}
\end{equation}
%
where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mu_i \in \mathbb{R}$ is the GEV location parameter,
  \item $\sigma_i > 0$ is the GEV scale parameter,
  \item $\xi_i \in \mathbb{R}$ is the GEV shape parameter ($\xi > 0$:
    heavy-tailed Fr\'{e}chet; $\xi = 0$: Gumbel; $\xi < 0$: bounded Weibull),
  \item $u_i$ is the station-specific threshold (we use the 75th percentile
    of positive daily precipitation at each station).
\end{itemize}

The PPP unifies the exceedance rate and tail behaviour in a single likelihood:
the third term in~\eqref{eq:ppp_loglik} governs the expected number of
exceedances per year, while the second term models the tail of the exceedance
magnitudes. The GEV parameters $(\mu_i, \sigma_i, \xi_i)$ map directly to
those of the annual maxima distribution.

Observations at different stations are modelled as \textbf{conditionally
independent} given the GEV parameters; spatial dependence enters through the
latent level.

\designnote{This conditional independence assumption is standard for marginal
return level estimation. Modelling joint extremes would require max-stable or
r-Pareto processes --- a possible extension.}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Latent Model and Parameterisation}
\label{sec:latent_model}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Following \citet{hazra2023blgm}, the GEV parameters are reparameterised as:
%
\begin{align}
  \psi_i &= \log(\mu_i)
  \label{eq:psi} \\[4pt]
  \tau_i &= \log(\sigma_i / \mu_i)
  \label{eq:tau} \\[4pt]
  \phi_i &= h(\xi_i) = a_\phi + b_\phi \log\!\big(-\log(1 - (\xi_i + 0.5)^{c_\phi})\big)
  \label{eq:phi}
\end{align}
%
where $c_\phi = 0.8$, and $a_\phi$, $b_\phi$ are constants chosen so that
$h$ maps $\xi \in (-0.5, 0.5)$ to $\phi \in \mathbb{R}$, with inverse
$g(\phi) = [1 - \exp(-\exp((\phi - a_\phi)/b_\phi))]^{1/c_\phi} - 0.5$.
The bounded transformation prevents pathologically heavy or bounded tails.

The latent model for the transformed parameters is:
%
\begin{align}
  \psi_i &= \beta_\psi + w_\psi(\mathbf{s}_i)
    + \varepsilon_i^\psi, \qquad
    \varepsilon_i^\psi \sim N(0, \nu_\psi^2)
  \label{eq:latent_psi} \\[4pt]
  \tau_i &= \beta_\tau + w_\tau(\mathbf{s}_i)
    + \varepsilon_i^\tau, \qquad
    \varepsilon_i^\tau \sim N(0, \nu_\tau^2)
  \label{eq:latent_tau} \\[4pt]
  \phi_i &= \beta_\phi + w_\phi(\mathbf{s}_i)
    + \varepsilon_i^\phi, \qquad
    \varepsilon_i^\phi \sim N(0, \nu_\phi^2)
  \label{eq:latent_phi}
\end{align}
%
where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\beta_\psi$, $\beta_\tau$, $\beta_\phi$ are intercepts,
  \item $w_\psi(\cdot)$, $w_\tau(\cdot)$, $w_\phi(\cdot)$ are zero-mean
    Gaussian process spatial random effects with Mat\'{e}rn(5/2)
    covariance (Section~\ref{sec:gp}),
  \item $\nu_\psi^2$, $\nu_\tau^2$, $\nu_\phi^2$ are nugget variances.
\end{itemize}

\designnote{The book chapter \citep{hazra2023blgm} models $\phi$ with
intercept $+$ nugget only (no spatial GP), arguing that $\xi$ shows little
spatial structure. We include a GP for $\phi$ as well, letting the data
decide. The posterior confirms weak spatial dependence for $\phi$:
$\hat{\sigma}_\phi = 0.12$ (small marginal SD) and
$\hat{\rho}_\phi = 3.6^{\circ}$ (very long range, effectively near-constant),
consistent with the book's finding but achieved without imposing the
constraint \emph{a priori}.}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Mat\'{e}rn(5/2) Gaussian Process}
\label{sec:gp}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each spatial random effect $w_p(\cdot)$ ($p \in \{\psi, \tau, \phi\}$)
is a zero-mean Gaussian process with a Mat\'{e}rn covariance function
of smoothness $\nu = 5/2$:
%
\begin{equation}
  C_p(\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{s}') = \sigma_p^2
    \left(1 + \frac{\sqrt{5}\,d}{\rho_p}
    + \frac{5\,d^2}{3\,\rho_p^2}\right)
    \exp\!\left(-\frac{\sqrt{5}\,d}{\rho_p}\right)
  \label{eq:matern52}
\end{equation}
%
where $d = \|\mathbf{s} - \mathbf{s}'\|$ is the Euclidean distance
(in degrees), $\sigma_p$ is the marginal standard deviation, and $\rho_p$
is the practical range parameter.

The joint prior on the spatial effects at the $n = 127$ station locations is:
%
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{w}_p = (w_p(\mathbf{s}_1), \ldots, w_p(\mathbf{s}_n))^\top
  \sim N(\mathbf{0},\, \mathbf{K}_p)
  \label{eq:gp_prior}
\end{equation}
%
where $[\mathbf{K}_p]_{ij} = C_p(\mathbf{s}_i, \mathbf{s}_j)$. We use
a non-centred parameterisation $\mathbf{w}_p = \mathbf{L}_p
\boldsymbol{\eta}_p^{\mathrm{raw}}$, $\boldsymbol{\eta}_p^{\mathrm{raw}}
\sim N(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{I})$, where $\mathbf{L}_p$ is the Cholesky
factor of $\mathbf{K}_p$, to improve MCMC sampling efficiency.

\designnote{The original book chapter \citep{hazra2023blgm} uses the SPDE
approach of \citet{lindgren2011spde} with Mat\'{e}rn($\nu = 1$) fields
discretised on a triangular mesh, yielding a GMRF representation. Our
direct GP approach with Mat\'{e}rn(5/2) operates on the $127 \times 127$
dense covariance matrix, which is feasible at this problem size and avoids
mesh-construction choices. The higher smoothness ($\nu = 5/2$ vs $\nu = 1$)
produces smoother spatial fields, which is appropriate for slowly-varying
climatological parameters.}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Return Levels}
\label{sec:return_levels}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

The $p$-year return level at station~$i$ (or any predicted location) is the
quantile:
%
\begin{equation}
  z_p(\mathbf{s}_i) = \mu_i + \frac{\sigma_i}{\xi_i}
    \left[ \left(-\log\!\left(1 - \frac{1}{p}\right)\right)^{-\xi_i}
    - 1 \right]
  \label{eq:return_level}
\end{equation}
%
Because $(\mu_i, \sigma_i, \xi_i)$ are functions of latent Gaussian variables
with full posterior distributions, we obtain posterior distributions for
$z_p(\mathbf{s}_i)$ at every location --- including credible intervals and
exceedance probabilities.

% =============================================================================
\section{Data}
\label{sec:data}
% =============================================================================

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Primary Source: AEMET Rain Gauge Network}
\label{sec:aemet}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Daily precipitation data are obtained from the Agencia Estatal de
Meteorolog\'{\i}a (AEMET) via the OpenData API, accessed through the
\texttt{climaemet} R package \citep{climaemet2026}. Key details:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Access:} Free API key from
    \url{https://opendata.aemet.es/centrodedescargas/obtencionAPIKey}
  \item \textbf{Variables:} Daily 24-hour accumulated precipitation (mm),
    station coordinates, elevation, province
  \item \textbf{Coverage:} $\sim$947 stations across Spain; to be filtered for
    Andaluc\'{\i}a (provinces: M\'{a}laga, Sevilla, C\'{o}rdoba, Granada,
    Ja\'{e}n, Almer\'{\i}a, C\'{a}diz, Huelva)
  \item \textbf{Record length:} Oldest stations from $\sim$1960; no
    minimum record length is imposed --- the Bayesian spatial model borrows
    strength across stations, so even short-record stations contribute
    useful spatial information
  \item \textbf{Quality control:} Basic QC provided by AEMET; we apply
    additional checks (negative values, implausible extremes, excessive
    missing data)
\end{itemize}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Covariates}
\label{sec:covariates}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Candidate spatial covariates for the latent model:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Elevation} (m a.s.l.) --- from station metadata or a DEM
    (e.g., SRTM 90\,m, EU-DEM 25\,m)
  \item \textbf{Distance to coast} (km) --- computed from station coordinates
    and a Mediterranean/Atlantic coastline shapefile
  \item \textbf{Orographic index} --- a measure of topographic exposure to
    moisture-bearing winds; computed from the DEM as the mean gradient in the
    dominant wind direction
  \item \textbf{Longitude / latitude} --- to capture large-scale
    east--west and north--south gradients in precipitation regime
\end{enumerate}

\todo{Jes\'{u}s to advise on additional covariates relevant to M\'{a}laga's
microclimate: aspect relative to the Cordillera B\'{e}tica, distance to the
Strait of Gibraltar, etc.}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Supplementary Data Sources}
\label{sec:supplementary_data}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\begin{description}[style=nextline]
  \item[\textbf{AEMET ROCIO} (5\,km grid, 1951--present)]
    Official AEMET gridded product, optimal interpolation from 3{,}236 stations.
    Useful for spatial context and cross-validation, not as the primary response.

  \item[\textbf{SPREAD} (5\,km grid, 1950--2012)]
    Geostatistical interpolation with per-cell uncertainty estimates. Useful
    for comparison and uncertainty propagation studies.

  \item[\textbf{ERA5-Land} (9\,km, 1950--present)]
    ECMWF reanalysis. Underestimates extremes over Spain, but valuable as a
    source of large-scale atmospheric covariates (e.g., moisture flux,
    synoptic typing for DANA identification).

  \item[\textbf{SAIH Hidrosur}]
    Real-time automatic rain gauges for the internal basins of Andaluc\'{\i}a.
    Useful for validating recent extreme events but shorter records.
\end{description}

