\part{Implementation}

% =============================================================================
\section{R Environment and Packages}
\label{sec:packages}
% =============================================================================

<<setup, include=FALSE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  echo    = TRUE,
  message = FALSE,
  warning = FALSE,
  fig.width  = 8,
  fig.height = 5,
  fig.path   = "figures/",
  cache   = TRUE,
  cache.path = "cache/"
)
@

<<packages, eval=FALSE>>=
# ---- Data acquisition ----
library(climaemet)        # AEMET OpenData API wrapper
library(sf)               # spatial features
library(terra)            # raster handling

# ---- Modelling (Max-and-Smooth) ----
library(cmdstanr)         # Stan interface (Stage 2 MCMC)
library(INLA)             # mesh construction (Stage 1 only)
library(Matrix)           # sparse matrix algebra
library(spam)             # sparse Cholesky decomposition
library(fields)           # distance matrices
library(coda)             # MCMC diagnostics
library(evd)              # classical GEV fitting (baseline)

# ---- Visualisation ----
library(ggplot2)
library(viridis)
library(patchwork)

# ---- General ----
library(tidyverse)
library(lubridate)
@

% =============================================================================
\section{Data Acquisition}
\label{sec:acquisition}
% =============================================================================

<<acquire-stations, eval=FALSE>>=
# ---- Step 1: Get AEMET station inventory ----
# Requires API key: aemet_api_key("YOUR_KEY", install = TRUE)

stations <- aemet_stations()

# Filter for Andalucia provinces
andalucia_provs <- c("MALAGA", "SEVILLA", "CORDOBA", "GRANADA",
                      "JAEN", "ALMERIA", "CADIZ", "HUELVA")

stations_and <- stations %>%
  filter(provincia %in% andalucia_provs) %>%
  st_as_sf(coords = c("longitud", "latitud"), crs = 4326)

cat("Stations in Andalucia:", nrow(stations_and), "\n")
@

<<acquire-daily, eval=FALSE>>=
# ---- Step 2: Download daily precipitation ----
# NOTE: AEMET API is rate-limited (50 requests/min).
# Download in batches by station and date range.

# Example: download for all Malaga stations, 1980-2024
malaga_daily <- aemet_daily_clim(
  station  = stations_and$indicativo[stations_and$provincia == "MALAGA"],
  start    = "1980-01-01",
  end      = "2024-12-31",
  return_sf = TRUE
)
@

<<compute-annual-maxima, eval=FALSE>>=
# ---- Step 3: Compute annual block maxima ----
annual_max <- daily_all %>%
  mutate(year = year(fecha)) %>%
  group_by(indicativo, year) %>%
  summarise(
    max_prec  = max(prec, na.rm = TRUE),
    n_obs     = sum(!is.na(prec)),
    .groups   = "drop"
  ) %>%
  # QC: require 90% completeness and finite max
  filter(n_obs >= 330, is.finite(max_prec), max_prec >= 0)

# No minimum-year threshold: the Bayesian spatial model borrows
# strength across stations, so even short-record stations contribute.

cat("Stations:", n_distinct(annual_max$indicativo), "\n")
cat("Total station-year observations:", nrow(annual_max), "\n")
@

The acquisition scripts (\texttt{R/01\_acquire\_aemet\_data.R}) have been run.
Key results:
\begin{itemize}
  \item 1{,}067{,}851 daily precipitation records downloaded (1960--2025)
  \item 127 stations across all 8 Andaluc\'{\i}an provinces after QC
    (Almer\'{\i}a~16, C\'{a}diz~15, C\'{o}rdoba~15, Granada~13,
     Huelva~15, Ja\'{e}n~13, M\'{a}laga~24, Sevilla~16)
  \item No minimum record length imposed: the Bayesian spatial model borrows
    strength
\end{itemize}

% =============================================================================
\section{Exploratory Analysis}
\label{sec:eda}
% =============================================================================

EDA is implemented in \texttt{R/02\_exploratory\_analysis.R} and produces
8 diagnostic figures. Key findings:
\begin{itemize}
  \item All at-site GEV shape parameters $\hat{\xi} > 0$ (Fr\'{e}chet-type,
    heavy tails), ranging from 0.04 (Sevilla, Granada) to 0.27 (C\'{o}rdoba)
  \item Strong spatial gradients: M\'{a}laga Airport has the highest
    $\hat{\mu} = 56$ mm; Grazalema (C\'{a}diz) is the overall wettest site
  \item Empirical variogram of annual maxima shows clear spatial dependence
    up to $\sim$200\,km
  \item Positive elevation--maxima relationship, consistent with orographic
    enhancement
\end{itemize}

% =============================================================================
\section{Max-and-Smooth Model Fitting}
\label{sec:ms_fitting}
% =============================================================================

The M\&S analysis is implemented in two stages:
Stage~1 in \texttt{R/03\_max\_and\_smooth.R} using functions from the
companion code to \citet{hazra2023blgm} (\texttt{vendor/max\_and\_smooth/}),
and Stage~2 in \texttt{R/03d\_smooth\_matern\_pc.R} using a custom
Stan program (\texttt{Stan/smooth\_matern\_pc.stan}).

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Stage~1: Site-wise Poisson Point Process MLEs}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

For each of the 127 stations, we:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Set the threshold $u_i$ as the 75th percentile of positive daily
    precipitation (range: 3.0--23.9\,mm, median 8.4\,mm).
  \item Maximise the PPP log-likelihood~\eqref{eq:ppp_loglik} to obtain MLEs
    $(\hat{\psi}_i, \hat{\tau}_i, \hat{\phi}_i)$.
  \item Compute the covariance matrix of the estimators via 1{,}000
    parametric bootstrap samples.
\end{enumerate}

All 127 stations converge. The MLEs on the original scale are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\hat{\mu}$: 20.0--131.0\,mm (median 42.6\,mm)
  \item $\hat{\sigma}$: 6.6--36.9\,mm (median 13.5\,mm)
  \item $\hat{\xi}$: $-0.026$ to $0.312$ (median 0.104)
\end{itemize}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Stage~2: MCMC Smoothing with Mat\'{e}rn GP (Stan)}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Stage~2 fits the Gaussian surrogate likelihood to the Stage~1 MLEs,
incorporating Mat\'{e}rn(5/2) GP spatial random effects for all three
transformed parameters ($\psi$, $\tau$, $\phi$). The model is implemented
in Stan \citep{carpenter2017stan} using the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS):
\begin{itemize}
  \item 4 chains $\times$ 2{,}000 iterations (1{,}000 warmup $+$
    1{,}000 sampling) $= 4{,}000$ posterior draws
  \item \texttt{adapt\_delta} $= 0.9$, \texttt{max\_treedepth} $= 12$
  \item Non-centred parameterisation for the GP (see Section~\ref{sec:gp})
  \item Runtime: $\sim$130 minutes on 4 CPU cores
  \item Divergences: 112/4{,}000 (3\%), spread evenly across chains;
    all $\hat{R} \leq 1.01$
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{PC priors.} We use penalised complexity priors
\citep{simpson2017pc, fuglstad2019pc} on all hyperparameters, calibrated
via probability statements:
\begin{itemize}
  \item GP marginal SD: $\sigma_p \sim \mathrm{Exp}(\lambda_\sigma)$ with
    $\lambda_\sigma = 3.0$, i.e.\ $P(\sigma_p > 1) = 0.05$
  \item GP range: $\pi(\rho_p) = \lambda_\rho \, \rho_p^{-2} \,
    \exp(-\lambda_\rho / \rho_p)$ with $\lambda_\rho = 0.30$, i.e.\
    $P(\rho_p < 0.1^{\circ}) = 0.05$
  \item Nugget SD: $\nu_p \sim \mathrm{Exp}(\lambda_\nu)$ with
    $\lambda_\nu = 6.0$, i.e.\ $P(\nu_p > 0.5) = 0.05$
  \item Intercepts: $\beta_p \sim N(0, 100^2)$ (weakly informative)
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Posterior hyperparameter summaries:}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrl}
\toprule
Parameter & Posterior mean & 95\% CI & Interpretation \\
\midrule
$\beta_\psi$ & 3.67 & (3.41, 3.87) & Intercept for $\log(\mu)$ \\
$\beta_\tau$ & $-1.04$ & ($-1.33$, $-0.66$) & Intercept for $\log(\sigma/\mu)$ \\
$\beta_\phi$ & 0.18 & (0.01, 0.49) & Intercept for shape \\[4pt]
$\sigma_\psi$ & 0.26 & (0.18, 0.41) & GP marginal SD for $\psi$ \\
$\sigma_\tau$ & 0.23 & (0.12, 0.49) & GP marginal SD for $\tau$ \\
$\sigma_\phi$ & 0.12 & (0.03, 0.40) & GP marginal SD for $\phi$ \\[4pt]
$\rho_\psi$ & 0.70 & (0.45, 1.07) & GP range for $\psi$ (degrees) \\
$\rho_\tau$ & 1.65 & (0.99, 2.75) & GP range for $\tau$ (degrees) \\
$\rho_\phi$ & 3.61 & (0.98, 10.88) & GP range for $\phi$ (degrees) \\[4pt]
$\nu_\psi$ & 0.150 & (0.125, 0.179) & Nugget SD for $\psi$ \\
$\nu_\tau$ & 0.011 & (0.002, 0.019) & Nugget SD for $\tau$ \\
$\nu_\phi$ & 0.003 & ($<$0.001, 0.007) & Nugget SD for $\phi$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\designnote{The GP range for $\phi$ ($\rho_\phi = 3.6^{\circ}$, 95\% CI:
0.98--10.9$^{\circ}$) is poorly constrained, with a posterior that wanders
to values exceeding the study domain diameter ($\sim$6$^{\circ}$). Combined
with the small marginal SD ($\sigma_\phi = 0.12$), this
confirms that the shape parameter has minimal spatial structure --- the GP
for $\phi$ is effectively fitting a near-constant surface. This is
consistent with the book chapter's decision to omit the spatial GP for
$\phi$.}

\paragraph{Prior sensitivity.} We also fitted the model with weakly
informative priors (Inverse-Gamma for range, Exponential for SD) instead
of PC priors. The posteriors for $\psi$ and $\tau$ are virtually identical
under both prior specifications; only the weakly-identified $\phi$
hyperparameters shift. Station-level 100-year return levels differ by
$< 1$\,mm, confirming that the results are robust to the choice of prior.
The PC prior model is retained as the primary specification due to its
principled construction and connection to the reference methodology.

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Smoothing Effect}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Spatial smoothing substantially reduces the variability of the parameter
estimates compared to the site-wise MLEs:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mu$: MLE range 20.0--131.0 $\rightarrow$ smoothed 20.8--128.7\,mm
  \item $\sigma$: MLE range 6.6--36.9 $\rightarrow$ smoothed 7.3--38.6\,mm
  \item $\xi$: MLE range $-0.026$ to $0.312$ $\rightarrow$ smoothed 0.065 to
    0.215
\end{itemize}

The strongest smoothing effect is on the shape parameter, where 127 noisy
site-wise estimates are pulled toward a common value of $\xi \approx 0.10$
(median 0.097), consistent with the relatively homogeneous heavy-tailed
(Fr\'{e}chet-type) precipitation regime across Andaluc\'{\i}a.

% =============================================================================
\section{Results: Return Level Maps}
\label{sec:results}
% =============================================================================

Return levels are computed from the posterior chains using
equation~\eqref{eq:return_level}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
Return period & Range of posterior mean (mm) & Range of posterior SD (mm) \\
\midrule
20-year  & 48.4 -- 260.2 & 2.1 -- 12.1 \\
50-year  & 59.2 -- 309.3 & 2.8 -- 16.8 \\
100-year & 68.3 -- 348.9 & 3.5 -- 21.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The Mediterranean coast shows the highest extremes overall, while inland
Granada and semi-arid Almer\'{\i}a have the lowest. By province, median
100-year return levels are: M\'{a}laga (158\,mm), C\'{a}diz (136\,mm),
Huelva (129\,mm), Sevilla (116\,mm), Almer\'{\i}a (104\,mm), C\'{o}rdoba
(103\,mm), Ja\'{e}n (98\,mm), Granada (97\,mm).

Figure~\ref{fig:interpolated} shows the posterior predictive interpolation
maps for the GEV parameters and 100-year return level, produced via
kriging on a $0.025^{\circ}$ grid covering Andaluc\'{\i}a.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/interpolated_maps_pc.png}
  \caption{Posterior predictive interpolation maps (Mat\'{e}rn GP + PC
    priors). Top row: GEV location ($\hat{\mu}$, mm), scale
    ($\hat{\sigma}$, mm), and shape ($\hat{\xi}$). Bottom row: 100-year
    return level (posterior mean, mm), posterior SD (mm), and coefficient
    of variation. Hollow circles indicate station locations.}
  \label{fig:interpolated}
\end{figure}

\todo{Extend the discussion:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Compare M\&S return levels against at-site GEV fits at long-record
    stations to quantify the benefit of spatial borrowing
  \item Cross-validation: leave-one-station-out predictive check
  \item Add covariates (elevation, distance to coast) to the latent model
  \item Discuss implications for infrastructure design thresholds in M\'{a}laga
  \item Relax completeness filter (330~$\to$~280 days) with season-aware
    criterion; 19 rejected station-years have annual max $>$~100\,mm
  \item Non-stationary GEV: time-varying $\mu(t)$ or $\sigma(t)$ to account
    for climate trends (current model assumes stationarity 1960--2025)
\end{itemize}}

% =============================================================================
% APPENDIX: PROPOSED JOINT DAILY--GEV MODEL
% =============================================================================

\newpage
\appendix
\section{Proposed extension: joint daily--GEV model}
\label{sec:joint-model}

The current Max-and-Smooth analysis treats annual block maxima as the
fundamental data unit. This appendix outlines a proposed extension that models
\emph{daily} rainfall directly, enabling principled imputation of missing
observations and a tighter link between the daily rainfall process and the
extreme value model.

\subsection{Motivation}

Our 90\% completeness filter (requiring $\geq 330$ daily observations per
station-year) discards 528 of 3029 station-years (17\%), including 19 with
annual maxima exceeding 100\,mm. Analysis of the missing days shows that
gaps are distributed across the calendar year (not concentrated in summer),
so some informative extreme events may be lost. The core question is: if a
station was offline during October (peak DANA season), what might we be
missing?

A secondary motivation is that the GEV shape parameter~$\xi$ --- which
governs tail heaviness and is our most uncertain parameter --- is currently
estimated from $\sim$2500 annual maxima. A daily model would inform $\xi$
from $\sim$1\,000\,000 daily observations, dramatically sharpening the
tail estimate.

\subsection{Model structure}

The proposed model has a modular structure with a one-directional dependency:
the daily model is estimated first (or jointly), and the GEV emerges as a
derived consequence.

\paragraph{Layer 1: Daily rainfall process.}
For station~$s$ on day~$t$, daily precipitation follows a hurdle model:
\begin{align}
  P(Y_{s,t} > 0) &= \mathrm{logit}^{-1}\bigl(\alpha_0(s) + f_p(d(t), s)\bigr),
    \label{eq:hurdle-occ} \\
  Y_{s,t} \mid Y_{s,t} > 0 &\sim \mathrm{GPD}\bigl(\sigma(s, d(t)),\;
    \xi(s)\bigr) \quad \text{for } Y_{s,t} > u(s),
    \label{eq:gpd-tail}
\end{align}
where $d(t)$ is the day-of-year (1--365), $f_p(\cdot)$ is a smooth seasonal
function (e.g.\ Fourier harmonics or cyclic spline), $u(s)$ is a high
threshold (e.g.\ the 95th percentile of wet-day amounts), and the
Generalized Pareto Distribution (GPD) models exceedances above~$u$.

Below the threshold, positive rainfall amounts can be modelled by a simpler
distribution (e.g.\ Gamma), since only the tail matters for extremes.

\paragraph{Layer 2: Shared spatial fields.}
All spatially varying parameters are given Mat\'{e}rn(5/2) GP priors, as in
the current model:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\xi(s) \sim \mathrm{GP}$ --- the tail shape, shared between the
    GPD (daily tail) and the implied GEV (block maxima)
  \item $\sigma(s, d(t))$ --- GPD scale with seasonal modulation
  \item $\alpha_0(s) \sim \mathrm{GP}$ --- baseline wet-day probability
  \item $f_p(d(t), s)$ --- seasonal component, borrowing strength across
    nearby stations
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Layer 3: The GEV--GPD link.}
The Pickands--Balkema--de~Haan theorem guarantees that if daily exceedances
follow a $\mathrm{GPD}(\sigma, \xi)$ with exceedance rate~$\lambda$ per
block, the block maximum converges to a $\mathrm{GEV}(\mu, \tilde\sigma,
\xi)$ with:
\begin{equation}
  \xi_{\mathrm{GEV}} = \xi_{\mathrm{GPD}}, \qquad
  \tilde\sigma = \sigma \, \lambda^{\xi}, \qquad
  \mu = u + \frac{\sigma}{\xi}(\lambda^{\xi} - 1).
  \label{eq:gev-gpd-link}
\end{equation}
This means the GEV parameters are \emph{derived quantities} --- not
separately estimated --- and the shape parameter~$\xi$ is informed by all
daily tail observations, not just annual maxima.

\paragraph{Layer 4: Missing data imputation.}
Within each MCMC iteration:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Sample missing daily values from the posterior predictive of the
    hurdle model, conditional on the current seasonal--spatial parameters
    and the observed days at that station and its neighbours.
  \item Recompute block maxima from observed~$+$ imputed daily values. Some
    imputed values may exceed the observed annual maximum, properly reflecting
    the uncertainty from incomplete records.
  \item Evaluate the GEV likelihood (or derive return levels directly from
    the augmented daily series).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Implementation plan}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Phase 1: Single-station prototype.} Fit the hurdle + GPD
    model to M\'{a}laga Aeropuerto (66~years, 23\,190 daily obs) in Stan.
    Validate the seasonal cycle and compare the implied GEV against the
    current station-level MLE.
  \item \textbf{Phase 2: Verify the GEV--GPD link.} From the fitted daily
    model, derive GEV parameters via~\eqref{eq:gev-gpd-link} and compare
    with existing return level estimates.
  \item \textbf{Phase 3: Multi-station spatial model.} Extend to all 127
    stations with shared GP priors on $\xi(s)$, $\sigma(s)$, and the
    seasonal parameters.
  \item \textbf{Phase 4: Missing data integration.} Impute missing days
    within the MCMC loop; compare return levels with and without imputation.
  \item \textbf{Phase 5: Non-stationarity.} Add a time trend to
    $\mu(t)$ or $\sigma(t)$ to model climate change effects on extremes.
\end{enumerate}

\designnote{The daily model is estimated independently of (or jointly with)
the GEV --- the dependency is one-directional. The daily process has
$\sim$1\,000\,000 observations to learn from, so allowing the GEV to
feed back into the daily model (a fully joint approach) would provide
only marginal additional information. The modular approach is therefore
a good approximation and substantially simpler to implement.}

